  "response": "# Triage of the `crimes scan` results for 03-node-cli-tool (51 findings)\n\n**Verdict: nothing here should block the merge.** I checked the key findings against the fixture source. Five groups of detector hits (37 of the 51 findings) are false positives. What's left is long-standing structural debt that's worth a warning. The highest `agent_risk` in the whole report is 0.46, and the three \"high\" severity findings are all size-based with very low agent_risk (0.09–0.15).\n\n## Block merge\nNone.\n\n## Warn (real, but fix in follow-ups)\n\n| ID | Finding | severity / agent_risk | Why warn |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 00033, 00034 | `choices()` is duplicated in `lib/argument.js:99-111` and `lib/option.js:176-188` | medium (0.55) / 0.18 | I confirmed the bodies match: same error text and same variadic handling. If one copy gets a fix (say, the error message), the other will drift. This is the most concrete maintenance risk in the report. Fix: a shared mixin or helper. |\n| 00036 | `lib/command.js` is 2179 lines (7.3× the threshold) | high (0.85) / 0.11 | Real context-window risk for agents editing it. Splitting it is a big job, so it doesn't belong in this PR. |\n| 00038 | `_executeSubCommand` is 120 lines | high (0.9) / 0.09 | It's the gnarliest method (spawn, signals, platform branches). Pull out helpers when someone next touches it. |\n| 00039, 00041, 00042 | `parseOptions` (115), `_parseCommand` (80), `addOption` (69) | medium (0.7) / 0.07–0.09 | Same story with lower priority. `addOption` is only 1.1× the threshold. |\n| 00035 | `typings/index.d.ts` is 891 lines | high (0.85) / 0.15 | This is a public type declaration file. It's large because the API surface is large, not because concerns are mixed. Warn, but splitting it isn't urgent. |\n| 00002 | `myParseInt` is duplicated across two `examples/` files | medium (0.55) / 0.33 | Example files are meant to be self-contained and copy-pasteable, so the duplication is defensible. I'm only flagging it because its agent_risk is the second highest. |\n\n## Ignore\n\n**False positives (checked against the source):**\n- **00003–00010, weak tests with \"no assertions\" (agent_risk 0.27 each).** The tests do assert. They call the helper `expectCommanderError()` (`tests/command.exitOverride.test.js:11-16`), which runs four `expect`s. The file even registers it with `jest/expect-expect` via `assertFunctionNames`. The detector doesn't follow assertion helpers.\n- **00011–00018, `ts-imports.test.ts` (0.27 each).** Same cause: the tests call `checkClass()`, which runs two `expect`s.\n- **00031, hardcoded `localhost:1234` (medium / 0.2).** `lib/command.js:2152` is a comment documenting the `--inspect=host:port` format that the regex parses. It isn't a live URL.\n- **00020, 00021, docs-code drift (0.24).** All three linked files exist in `docs/zh-CN/` (`术语表.md`, `可变参数的选项.md`, `不再推荐使用的功能.md`). The links are just percent-encoded, and the detector doesn't decode them.\n- **00032, commented-out code (0.19).** The two lines in `tests/command.executableSubcommand.search.test.js:13-14` describe the `spawn` call shapes the mock is unpacking. They're documentation, not dead code.\n\n**Low value:**\n- **00001, the `\"PORT\"` literal (medium / 0.46, the highest agent_risk).** It's an option or env name used independently in standalone examples and type tests. Each example is supposed to be self-contained, so pulling it into a constant would hurt them.\n- **00019, logic in a comment (low / 0.27).** It's a historical note about SIGUSR1, not an unencoded rule. The \"retry\" match comes from a quoted Node error message.\n- **00022–00030, weak matchers (low / 0.23).** These use `toBeTruthy`/`toBeFalsy` on boolean flags, after first asserting the opposite state. That's adequate. Tightening to `toBe(true)` is a nit.\n- **00037, 00043, 00044, files over 300 lines (0.06–0.11).** That's a type-test file, `help.js` (one cohesive concern), and `option.js` (1.1× the threshold).\n- **00040, 00045–00048, long `describe` callbacks in tests (0.04–0.08).** Test suites that run long are normal and low-risk.\n- **00049–00051, long docs (0.03–0.04).** A CHANGELOG and READMEs are long by nature.\n\n## Notes for the eval\n- The severity and `agent_risk` rankings disagree. The three \"high\" findings are among the lowest agent_risk in the report, while the top agent_risk finding (PORT, 0.46) is the most ignorable. I based the gate on what I verified in the code, not on either score alone.\n- Detector improvements worth filing: follow assertion helpers (or honour `jest/expect-expect` `assertFunctionNames`), percent-decode doc links, and skip `localhost` matches that sit inside comments.",
